issue(id="0010" status="closed")
  title Stress test aggressive whitespace compaction
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of puggers
    i-want a focused stress suite for whitespace compaction
    so-that we can measure how far normalization can go before semantics or readability break
  premise.
    Several future formatter improvements will be tempted to compact whitespace:
    collapsing redundant spaces, trimming blank lines, or minimizing
    statement-head and attribute-list separators. In Pug, those moves can be
    deceptively dangerous once they cross from structural whitespace into text
    payloads such as piped text, dotted blocks, inline tag text, and
    interpolation-heavy prose. A dedicated stress test task should push
    structural compaction hard on representative and adversarial fixtures so we
    can identify the exact boundaries where normalization stops being safe and
    where text payload policies must remain separate.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Add a fixture suite that exercises aggressive structural whitespace compaction across statement heads, attribute lists, comments, blank lines, and other grammar-closed separators.
    criterion Record which structural compaction transforms are safe, conditionally safe, or clearly unsafe, with examples for each category and with text-payload cases called out as out of scope for this task.
    criterion Include cases where compaction changes rendered output, not just source appearance, so regressions are obvious.
    criterion Produce a short summary of compaction findings that later formatting tasks can treat as policy input rather than rediscovering ad hoc.
  review-items
    item(status="covered") The stress suite now exercises hand-authored Pug cases around attribute separators, recoverable missing separators, inline trailing whitespace, and tab-driven structural normalization.
    item(status="covered") `docs/whitespace-policy.md` records which separator compactions are safe, conditional, or unsafe so later work can reuse the policy instead of rediscovering it.
    item(status="covered") Blank-line compaction is now validated across plain statements, nested blocks, comments, filters, dotted text, raw-text tags, and mixed-content files.
    item(status="covered") Repeated structural spaces and tabs in non-tag statement heads are now compacted and verified as part of this task's compaction policy.
